      "math_academy_id": "q-112435",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -3 \\\\  &  & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)(3{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $3{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(3)(1)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(0)^{3}-9(0)^{2}+0-3=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/7a04ed5e26758551d7eca9949a274884f28f43a001439b58e393470721d96e5d.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
